title: Owner and reach
description: Who owns a piece of configuration and how far it applies. Together they make the four cells that exist.
sections:
  - title: Owner
    description: Who writes it and who sees it. Three values.
    body: |
      | Owner | Where it lives | Examples |
      |---|---|---|
      | Org | Deployed by an admin to every machine or every account | A managed CLAUDE.md, managed-settings.json, provisioned skills |
      | Team | Committed in the repo, or a Project shared with people | The repo's CLAUDE.md, `.claude\rules`, `.claude\skills`, `.claude\settings.json`, a shared Project |
      | Me | My home folder, a git-ignored local file, or my own account | `~\.claude\CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude\settings.local.json`, auto-memory, Settings > General |

      "Global" and "local" are not places on this axis. Global means me everywhere. Local means me in this repo. Both are cells of owner times reach.
  - title: Reach
    description: How far it applies. A folder tree, everywhere, or one Project.
    body: |
      | Reach | Meaning |
      |---|---|
      | Everywhere | Every project on this machine, or the whole account |
      | A folder and everything below it | An ancestor folder, the repo root, a subfolder, or files matching a path pattern |
      | One Project | A claude.ai Project, in the chat app only |

      Project, repo, ancestor folder and subfolder are the same value at different depths. CLAUDE.md files stack along the path from the drive root down to the file being touched: ancestors load at start, a subfolder's file loads when Claude works there, and a path-scoped rule narrows reach further with a glob.
    children:
      - title: The levels of a folder tree
        body: |
          - **An ancestor folder.** `C:\Github\CLAUDE.md` applies to every repo under `C:\Github`, because Claude Code walks up from the working directory and loads each ancestor's CLAUDE.md.
          - **The repo root.** The main CLAUDE.md, plus `.claude\CLAUDE.md` as a second home for it.
          - **A subfolder.** `dates\CLAUDE.md` loads only when Claude reads or edits a file inside `dates`.
          - **A path pattern.** A rule whose frontmatter lists `paths:` globs attaches only when a matching file is touched.
  - title: The four populated cells
    description: Owner times reach has six cells in Claude Code. Only four have a mechanism.
    body: |
      | | Everywhere | A folder tree |
      |---|---|---|
      | Org | Managed CLAUDE.md, managed-settings.json, provisioned skills | Nothing |
      | Team | Nothing | CLAUDE.md at any level, `.claude\rules`, `.claude\skills`, `.claude\settings.json`, `.mcp.json` |
      | Me | `~\.claude\CLAUDE.md`, `~\.claude\rules`, `~\.claude\skills`, `~\.claude\settings.json` | `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude\settings.local.json`, auto-memory |

      These four are exactly the four folders of the claude-map mirror: org managed, me all projects, team this repo, me this project. The chat app adds the Project column: a shared Project is the team's, a private one is mine.
  - title: Gaps in the grid
    description: Cells with no mechanism, and what to do instead.
    body: |
      - **No personal rules or skills for a single repo.** Anything that is mine alone in one project has to be CLAUDE.local.md, settings.local.json or memory.
      - **Memory lives in exactly one cell,** me in this repo. That is why it is stored under the home folder but keyed by the project path, and why it never reaches teammates or cloud sessions. Moving a fact to the team is a change of cell, not a copy.
      - **Nothing a team writes applies everywhere.** Team-wide defaults across repos are the org's cell, or a plugin marketplace.
  - title: How reach differs per harness
    body: |
      In Claude Code reach is always a folder tree, with everywhere meaning the home folder. In the chat app reach is the account, a Project, or an anchored folder. The same owner values apply to both, which is what makes one grid per harness possible.
